WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.469 --> 00:00:05.404
-Everybody, welcome back.

00:00:05.404 --> 00:00:06.706
Let's just jump right into it.

00:00:06.706 --> 00:00:10.409
So there's always
one thing every week

00:00:10.409 --> 00:00:13.680
that we get
the most feedback on.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.916
This is actually from
yesterday's Closer Look.

00:00:16.916 --> 00:00:21.754
We had a satellite
explode in space,

00:00:21.754 --> 00:00:24.023
and the two things
we heard the most is,

00:00:24.023 --> 00:00:28.727
one, in space,
you would not hear an explosion,

00:00:28.727 --> 00:00:32.198
and in space,
when a satellite exploded,

00:00:32.198 --> 00:00:35.167
the debris wouldn't
fall straight down

00:00:35.167 --> 00:00:38.037
because of the lack of gravity.

00:00:38.037 --> 00:00:44.511
And it rankles me pretty bad,

00:00:44.511 --> 00:00:49.315
because when we hire people
into our Graphics department,

00:00:49.315 --> 00:00:50.884
the two questions
we ask them is

00:00:50.884 --> 00:00:52.718
one, do you know how
to do graphics?

00:00:52.718 --> 00:00:55.054
And two,

00:00:55.054 --> 00:00:57.090
do you understand
how space works?

00:00:59.859 --> 00:01:02.695
And somebody pointed it out,

00:01:02.695 --> 00:01:08.034
wrote, "Jackals: 1.
Graphic: 0,"

00:01:08.034 --> 00:01:10.637
and you just couldn't
be more wrong.

00:01:10.637 --> 00:01:15.308
They don't feel like this
is a defeat at all.

00:01:15.308 --> 00:01:19.145
This is how they flirt.

00:01:19.145 --> 00:01:21.080
They make mistakes on purpose,

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:24.884
the way someone leaves,
like, a perfumed handkerchief

00:01:24.884 --> 00:01:27.520
at a Victorian ball.

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:29.422
And then I'm stuck
going down there saying,

00:01:29.422 --> 00:01:32.758
"Guys, do you not know
the properties of space?"

00:01:32.758 --> 00:01:35.462
And they're like,
"Oh, no, did we get it wrong?

00:01:37.763 --> 00:01:41.468
Did someone find our mistake?"

00:01:43.569 --> 00:01:45.238
It's not just that.

00:01:45.238 --> 00:01:47.040
You know, we talked about it
last week.

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:51.944
We talked about
this problematic pigeon graphic

00:01:51.944 --> 00:01:59.018
where the nostrils were exposed,
and as so many of you said,

00:01:59.018 --> 00:02:02.322
that's, again,
the least of the problem.

00:02:02.322 --> 00:02:05.291
What wall has bricks
going that way?

00:02:07.693 --> 00:02:09.962
Who...? When have you...?

00:02:09.962 --> 00:02:12.364
And again, so that I'm
down there being like,

00:02:12.364 --> 00:02:13.666
"Guys, what is this?"

00:02:13.666 --> 00:02:17.303
And they're like, "Oh,
is that not how bricks work?

00:02:17.303 --> 00:02:20.640
Did somebody find it
and leave a comment?"

00:02:26.279 --> 00:02:30.315
Pete Rose did not ever
bet against the Reds.

00:02:30.315 --> 00:02:31.684
We had in the Closer Look

00:02:31.684 --> 00:02:35.288
that Pete Rose was on the phone
with his bookies,

00:02:35.288 --> 00:02:37.223
and people wanted
to make it very clear

00:02:37.223 --> 00:02:39.225
that he maybe did a lot of
things, he bet on baseball.

00:02:39.225 --> 00:02:41.261
He never
bet against his own team.

00:02:43.595 --> 00:02:45.231
Alright,
early days of corrections,

00:02:45.231 --> 00:02:46.899
we talked about
the correct way

00:02:46.899 --> 00:02:51.904
to pronounce
the Irish political position

00:02:51.904 --> 00:02:54.340
the Taoiseach,

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:56.476
which is like
the Irish prime minister.

00:02:56.476 --> 00:02:58.611
We spent a lot of time
talking about it.

00:02:58.611 --> 00:03:01.046
We went back and forth.

00:03:01.046 --> 00:03:04.650
Guys, this is a true thing
that happened.

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:12.725
Last week, the actual taoiseach
was in our studio

00:03:12.725 --> 00:03:15.728
and nobody got me.

00:03:17.863 --> 00:03:21.901
Nobody thought, "Oh, that might
be interesting to Seth,

00:03:21.901 --> 00:03:24.470
who's talked about it
at length."

00:03:26.472 --> 00:03:29.875
I mean, what in the world?

00:03:29.875 --> 00:03:31.677
That's like somebody saying,
"Yeah, no.

00:03:31.677 --> 00:03:33.212
Werner Herzog
and Christoph Waltz

00:03:33.212 --> 00:03:34.814
were in the studio,

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:39.084
but we didn't want to get you.

00:03:39.084 --> 00:03:40.452
We thought you were busy."

00:03:40.452 --> 00:03:41.888
They sound very different.

00:03:45.425 --> 00:03:48.294
So then I was, you know,
obviously, I'm pretty angry

00:03:48.294 --> 00:03:50.429
and I'm like,
"Did nobody think to get me?"

00:03:50.429 --> 00:03:52.531
And they said, "Well, we told
Graphics to tell you."

00:03:52.531 --> 00:03:55.301
And I'm like --

00:03:55.301 --> 00:03:56.736
I'm like, "Graphics, did you?"

00:03:56.736 --> 00:03:59.405
And they're like, "We slipped
a note under the door."

00:03:59.405 --> 00:04:00.606
And I'm like, "My door?"

00:04:00.606 --> 00:04:05.878
They're like, "No, just a door."

00:04:08.347 --> 00:04:12.084
People have been
lamenting the fact

00:04:12.084 --> 00:04:14.920
that for as much as
he's talked about,

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:17.823
Shoemaker is never on camera,

00:04:17.823 --> 00:04:22.895
and that it would, I don't know,
enrich this experience

00:04:22.895 --> 00:04:24.796
if they knew
what Shoemaker looked like.

00:04:24.796 --> 00:04:25.798
He's not,
but here's the thing --

00:04:25.798 --> 00:04:26.999
he doesn't want to be on camera.

00:04:26.999 --> 00:04:29.135
He's not getting on camera,
so I will just --

00:04:29.135 --> 00:04:31.704
just to paint
a picture for you.

00:04:31.704 --> 00:04:37.343
He is a fairly nondescript,
middle-aged man

00:04:37.343 --> 00:04:42.582
with the most
lustrous head of hair.

00:04:44.783 --> 00:04:49.154
Like, just long,
down to the shoulders,

00:04:49.154 --> 00:04:55.394
just like,
a beautiful, blond mane.

00:04:55.394 --> 00:04:57.729
And he's always like, that's...

00:04:57.729 --> 00:05:00.366
I've done impressions of of him

00:05:00.366 --> 00:05:01.768
that I think
are fairly accurate,

00:05:01.768 --> 00:05:05.271
but that's the one thing
I do always leave out.

00:05:05.271 --> 00:05:07.640
Because he's always like,
"Who's asking?"

00:05:11.577 --> 00:05:18.117
And he's always talking about,
like, how he wants a new brush.

00:05:18.117 --> 00:05:19.952
He's like, "I was walking down
Fifth Avenue.

00:05:19.952 --> 00:05:22.755
I saw this gorgeous
antique brush,

00:05:25.891 --> 00:05:27.126
but I can't afford it.

00:05:27.126 --> 00:05:28.927
One day.

00:05:28.927 --> 00:05:32.565
One day, I'm going to get
that beautiful antique brush."

00:05:36.735 --> 00:05:40.005
Some people said
my Randy Newman,

00:05:40.005 --> 00:05:43.576
which I think
we've established is good,

00:05:43.576 --> 00:05:45.678
sounds like Bill Cosby.

00:05:48.714 --> 00:05:50.516
I don't know.

00:05:50.516 --> 00:05:53.319
[ As Randy Newman ]
"Roll down the window.

00:05:53.319 --> 00:05:56.789
Put down the top.

00:05:56.789 --> 00:05:59.825
Turn up the Beach Boys, baby.

00:05:59.825 --> 00:06:01.861
Hand me that
Jell-o Pudding Pop."

00:06:08.234 --> 00:06:11.870
Called Andy Samberg last week,
and I asked him to

00:06:11.870 --> 00:06:15.607
sing Fine Young Cannibals
for us.

00:06:15.607 --> 00:06:20.212
I said, "Sing us the song,
'She Drives You Crazy.'"

00:06:20.212 --> 00:06:24.050
People said the song is
"She Drives Me Crazy."

00:06:24.050 --> 00:06:25.817
That's what it is
when he's singing it,

00:06:25.817 --> 00:06:29.888
but if you're asking
another person to sing it,

00:06:29.888 --> 00:06:32.892
you switch the pronouns.

00:06:32.892 --> 00:06:35.027
So if I want him to sing,
"She Drives Me Crazy,"

00:06:35.027 --> 00:06:36.862
I have to say, sing
"She Drives You Crazy."

00:06:36.862 --> 00:06:42.101
That's just common sense.

00:06:42.101 --> 00:06:45.671
Also, people did not believe
it was a real call.

00:06:45.671 --> 00:06:46.972
It was a real call.

00:06:46.972 --> 00:06:49.275
I did not think
he was going to answer.

00:06:49.275 --> 00:06:52.011
I think we were
incredibly lucky last week.

00:06:52.011 --> 00:06:53.479
We'll try it again here.

00:06:53.479 --> 00:06:54.513
[ Cellphone rings ]

00:06:54.513 --> 00:06:57.049
See, that's real time,
right there.

00:06:57.049 --> 00:07:01.454
[ Cellphone ringing ]

00:07:01.454 --> 00:07:03.488
I guess we're not gonna
get him this week.

00:07:03.488 --> 00:07:06.526
[ Cellphone ringing ]

00:07:07.727 --> 00:07:10.129
That's how it works.
Corrections.

00:07:10.129 --> 00:07:11.764
It's like watching sports.

00:07:14.233 --> 00:07:15.968
It's the thrill of the live.

00:07:18.871 --> 00:07:23.809
I referred to the "Fast Five"
movie franchise.

00:07:23.809 --> 00:07:27.546
"Fast Five" was the name
of one of the movies.

00:07:27.546 --> 00:07:29.182
I don't know which one.

00:07:31.884 --> 00:07:35.654
I also said that if I were in
a "Fast and the Furious" movie,

00:07:35.654 --> 00:07:37.890
I would be a guy
holding out a coffee

00:07:37.890 --> 00:07:40.593
and then a car would
come by and be like, "Whoa!"

00:07:40.593 --> 00:07:41.894
And Vin Diesel would be like,

00:07:41.894 --> 00:07:43.895
"Oh, a [bleep] latte"
and throw it out the window.

00:07:43.895 --> 00:07:45.298
And people were like,

00:07:45.298 --> 00:07:47.299
"Hey, based on the way
you did that,

00:07:47.299 --> 00:07:49.201
he would either have to be
in the wrong lane

00:07:49.201 --> 00:07:51.003
or driving backwards."

00:07:51.003 --> 00:07:57.043
Does that not happen
in those movies?

00:07:57.043 --> 00:08:00.112
Because I'm pretty sure
in the trailers,

00:08:00.112 --> 00:08:02.447
Vin Diesel often says,
"Hold on,"

00:08:02.447 --> 00:08:03.583
and then just like, vroom.

00:08:07.986 --> 00:08:10.555
I did say that I also --
a good role for me in the movie

00:08:10.555 --> 00:08:14.760
would be carrying across,
like, a plate of glass.

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:17.363
And someone said,
"I love that Seth Meyers thinks

00:08:17.363 --> 00:08:20.465
the 'Fast and the Furious'
movies are like

00:08:20.465 --> 00:08:22.802
'70s car chase comedies."

00:08:24.837 --> 00:08:27.272
It's a very fair observation.

00:08:27.272 --> 00:08:31.143
So I think if I were in
a "Fast and the Furious" movie,

00:08:31.143 --> 00:08:33.178
I bet a good role for me
would be someone

00:08:33.178 --> 00:08:34.813
who would say something like,
"I get it.

00:08:34.813 --> 00:08:37.250
You're all a bunch of cowboys."

00:08:40.419 --> 00:08:42.521
I referred to "Lillehammer,"

00:08:42.521 --> 00:08:46.224
the Little Steven
show on Netflix,

00:08:46.224 --> 00:08:50.129
and people said, "Yes, that is
the name of the Norwegian city,

00:08:50.129 --> 00:08:52.698
but the show is called
"Lilyhammer,"

00:08:52.698 --> 00:08:54.767
because that was how
his character said it, so...

00:08:54.767 --> 00:08:56.168
Also, not a correction,

00:08:56.168 --> 00:08:57.936
but I feel like
we have underappreciated

00:08:57.936 --> 00:09:02.942
what a good actor
Little Steven is.

00:09:02.942 --> 00:09:05.077
So that would be like,
a correction to how

00:09:05.077 --> 00:09:06.411
well-received...

00:09:06.411 --> 00:09:11.483
I feel like we've underestimated
exactly how great he was.

00:09:11.483 --> 00:09:15.755
I referred to Pavarotti as
a B-list celebrity.

00:09:17.990 --> 00:09:24.629
Some opera enthusiasts
took issue with that, but

00:09:24.629 --> 00:09:27.966
I should have said --

00:09:27.966 --> 00:09:31.671
We should've made this clear,
when I say B-list

00:09:31.671 --> 00:09:34.540
about an Italian,
it means "bellissimo."

00:09:41.179 --> 00:09:45.918
You guys, if you haven't
noticed, I'm wearing

00:09:45.918 --> 00:09:47.553
sweater sleeves.

00:09:51.190 --> 00:09:52.825
So...

00:09:53.492 --> 00:09:57.163
[ Exhales deeply ]

00:09:57.163 --> 00:10:02.200
Last week, my worst mistake
was I referred to...

00:10:02.200 --> 00:10:08.874
something that Patty Lyons
sewed for me as a sweater.

00:10:08.874 --> 00:10:13.145
You guys all pointed out
that it was a sweater vest.

00:10:13.145 --> 00:10:16.448
And I --
It was a bad mistake,

00:10:16.448 --> 00:10:19.051
and I spent a lot of the week
pretty upset about it.

00:10:19.051 --> 00:10:22.688
Like, mad at myself
for missing something so easy,

00:10:22.688 --> 00:10:25.057
because it was clearly a sweater
vest, and I called it a sweater,

00:10:25.057 --> 00:10:29.828
And sometimes I am a little
bit harder on myself than

00:10:29.828 --> 00:10:33.799
I maybe present out here.

00:10:33.799 --> 00:10:37.069
And...

00:10:37.069 --> 00:10:39.538
I think Shoemaker noticed,

00:10:39.538 --> 00:10:45.277
because today he came in
with a gift box.

00:10:47.346 --> 00:10:50.149
And also, all his hair
had been cut off.

00:10:53.786 --> 00:10:57.790
And I said, "Oh my God, what?
Where's your hair?

00:10:57.790 --> 00:10:58.924
Where's your beautiful hair?"

00:10:58.924 --> 00:11:01.527
And he said,
"You know, this wig maker's

00:11:01.527 --> 00:11:04.095
always been asking me for it.

00:11:04.095 --> 00:11:05.731
Said it was the most
beautiful hair she'd ever seen.

00:11:05.731 --> 00:11:08.534
I sold it to her."

00:11:08.534 --> 00:11:10.636
And I was like,
"Sold it to her? Why?"

00:11:10.636 --> 00:11:13.072
And he said, "I wanted
to get you something,

00:11:13.072 --> 00:11:16.275
because I see
how down you've been."

00:11:16.275 --> 00:11:18.677
And he hands me this box,
and I open it up,

00:11:18.677 --> 00:11:21.347
and it's these sleeves.

00:11:21.347 --> 00:11:25.084
And he says,
"Now it's a sweater.

00:11:25.084 --> 00:11:27.787
You put that on the vest,
you walk out there,

00:11:27.787 --> 00:11:31.690
you show those jackals
who you are."

00:11:31.690 --> 00:11:33.426
And I said, "I can't.

00:11:35.494 --> 00:11:37.062
I can't."

00:11:37.062 --> 00:11:38.130
And when he asked why not,

00:11:38.130 --> 00:11:39.865
I said, "I..."

00:11:39.865 --> 00:11:41.299
[ Bag rustling ]

00:11:41.299 --> 00:11:44.737
"I sold the sweater
to get you this."

00:11:53.612 --> 00:12:00.385
I think --
I think that's everything.

00:12:00.385 --> 00:12:01.387
See me next week.

